messages:
anya: quick follow-up from the quota <> abuse surface call this morning. proposal sketch: unify the QuotaCatalog -> AbuseScore interaction so admission gets a single 'admit decision' + explainability headers. TL;DR: fewer RPC hops and clearer audit trail, but tighter coupling between scoring and quota decisions. thoughts?
dan: interesting. do you mean moving scoring into the quota-service or calling a standalone scorer synchronously?
anya: standalone scorer behind a thin shim. quota-service calls scoring sync for provisional admits. scoring outage -> fallback score (configurable, default 0.5) and conservative admission. want to avoid silent pass-through.
maria: +1 on fallback, but latency is the big risk. scoring must be local to avoid cross-dc RTTs. we should replicate model configs async to each region.
sean: coupling brings complexity around revokes and reconciliation. if a score changes after admit we need idempotent decision logs. we should attach a decision_id/debug_id to every admission.
anya: agree. api returns {score, explanation, model_version, debug_id}. quota-service logs model_version & debug_id with the lease so we can replay/reconcile.
dan: cost question: this adds another RPC per request. can we batch scoring inside the proxy or leverage existing kv/cache used for prompt prefix caching?
maria: batching helps for high-throughput non-LLL (long-latency) flows. for low-latency chat we should use an in-region LRU cache keyed by tenant+prompt_hash or client fingerprint. suggest per-tenant cache config (ttl, max).
anya: propose default cache TTL=5s and a tunable per-tenant override. also a 'safe-mode' where if scoring fails we apply a conservative weight multiplier on token charges so we don't undercharge abusive clients.
sean: migration plan idea: start with shadowing (quota-service calls scoring in shadow, refuses to act on the result), run for X days, compare distributions, then opt-in tenants via feature flag, then global flip. rollback = kill scoring calls in proxy.
dan: we should define observability: per-tenant and global score distribution, binned by rps and error-rates, plus correlation with api usage. dashboards + alerting thresholds.
anya: drafting a small proto snippet for the scorer interface in the RFC follow-up: 
```
service AbuseScorer {
  rpc Score(ScoreRequest) returns (ScoreReply) {}
}
message ScoreRequest {
  string tenant_id = 1;
  string request_id = 2; // guaranteed unique id from ingress
  bytes prompt_hash = 3; // opaque fingerprint
  map<string,string> metadata = 4; // optional signals (ip_region, plan, route)
}
message ScoreReply {
  double score = 1; // 0..1, higher == more likely abusive
  string model_version = 2;
  string debug_id = 3;
  repeated string explanations = 4;
}
```
maria: proto looks good. add a boolean `cached` or `cache_ttl_ms` to the reply so quota-service can reason about staleness.
sean: SLO discussion: scoring p50/p95 goals should be small for local (p95 < 10ms) and regional fallback p95 < 50ms. also cap error budget for scoring (e.g., 0.1% errors).
dan: ops impact: more alarms + a new eviction path when scoring is down. we need a playbook: scoring outage -> enable global conservative admission multiplier, page scorer oncall.
anya: i'll add those to the RFC's rollout checklist (shadow, per-tenant opt-in, dashboards, playbook, feature-flag kill-switch).
deploy-bot: canary deployed scorer v0.1 to staging (cluster: eu-west-1) :rocket:
maria: thanks, i'll start a short runbook for per-tenant config knobs (cache_ttl, safe_mode_multiplier, shadow_mode).
anya: ok i'm going to open the RFC follow-up with the api proto + migration plan + sample dashboards. will tag infra/observability and platform. thanks for quick takes — will sync in eng-security async if any blockers. :+1:
